    "lawCode": "Amd §409, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5627\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to the\nuse of school buildings for school age child care\n\nPURPOSE:  This bill provides that the provision of school age child\ncare in public school buildings shall primarily be subject to the\nregulations of the commissioner of education.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill adds a new subdivision 3 to section 409 of the\nEducation Law to preempt any contradictory regulations promulgated by\nanother state agency, relating to the use of a school building by a\nregistered third party providing school aged child care as defined in\nsubparagraph (f) of subdivision one of section 390 of the social\nservice law.\n\nSection 2 is the enacting clause.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  All youth deserve access to high-quality afterschool\nprograms. The State School-Age Child Care (SACC) regulations seek to\nensure the basic health and safety of children in child care and to\npromote their positive development. Unfortunately, afterschool\nprograms are challenged in their efforts to meet those licensing\nrequirements and, in many ways, the regulations create barriers to\nhigh-quality programming.\n\nSACC regulations are sometimes inconsistent with school building\nrequirements. As a result, afterschool programs operating in school\nbuildings are then penalized for issues outside of their control -\nissues that haven't been shown to pose any additional threat to\nstudents after the school day ends. School buildings that are deemed\nsafe for students from 8 am to 3 pm should be deemed safe for students\nfrom 3 pm to 6 pm.\n\nA school building is the responsibility of the school district that\nowns it, however, afterschool providers are made responsible for\nresolving those instances in which the building is out of compliance\nwith the SACC regulations. The time that program staff spends trying\nto get schools to update buildings is time that could have been spent\non developing high-quality programming and staff training. Putting the\nstaff in the uncomfortable position of asking the school administrator\nto bring the school into compliance with SACC regulations strains\ntheir relationship with the school.\n\nFor example, an afterschool program is held responsible for taking\ndown posters on the walls put up by teachers in their own classrooms\nbecause these posters are considered a fire hazard in the afterschool\nprogram based on the SACC regulations. School administrators do not\nconsider these posters a fire hazard - in fact they actively encourage\nthem during the school day - so they should not be considered a hazard\nafterschool. This has led to tensions between the program and\nteachers, and in some cases has resulted in teachers prohibiting the\nafterschool program from using their classrooms during afterschool\nhours because they do not want their posters moved.\n\n\nThis bill offers a common sense solution to these issues Keep and\nrespect the SACC regulations, waving only those that affect the\nphysical plant.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:. None\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: Minimal\n\nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
